>
  <h2>TL;DR: define the service and the lead handoff before you scale</h2>
  <p>
    An established practice should expect a Facebook and Instagram advertising partner to define
    account access, the campaign goal, offer, creative approvals, conversion location, lead
    delivery, reporting, and the office's response responsibilities. ChiroCandy manages Meta ads
    for chiropractic practices; the exact placements, assets, destinations, integrations, fees,
    and practice responsibilities should be confirmed in the current proposal and account review.
  </p>
  <p>
    Ads Manager can report delivery and configured conversion events. It cannot, by itself, prove
    that an inquiry was relevant, an appointment was booked or held, or a patient or revenue
    outcome occurred. The useful buying question is whether the campaign and office workflow form
    one reviewable system.
  </p>

  <h2>What should a chiropractic Meta Ads engagement define?</h2>
  <p>
    Use this scope map to compare providers and turn a proposal into named responsibilities. A
    workstream may not apply to every practice, but the written scope should say whether it is
    included, excluded, or owned by someone else.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Facebook and Instagram advertising scope and evidence">
    <table>
      <caption>Campaign scope, ownership, and review evidence</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Workstream</th><th scope="col">Define before launch</th><th scope="col">Review with evidence</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Account and access</th><td>Business owner, administrators, agency access, billing, connected assets, and exit handoff</td><td>Observed permissions, billing owner, asset list, and written transition process</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Goal and offer</th><td>Promoted service, market, offer terms, appointment capacity, decision owner, and spending boundary</td><td>Approved offer, actual capacity, and a shared definition of the business outcome</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Creative and approval</th><td>Copy, image or video source, versions, brand facts, permissions, claim reviewer, and final approver</td><td>Approved asset, approval record, current policy check, and replacement plan</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Campaign setup</th><td>Objective, conversion location, performance goal, audience and geography controls, placements, and exclusions</td><td>Current account settings, delivery breakdowns, change history, and documented limitations</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Destination and delivery</th><td>Instant form, website, call, message or booking path; required fields; notification recipients; and backup</td><td>Approved non-patient test, receipt timestamp, assigned owner, and correct destination</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Reporting and decisions</th><td>Media spend, fees, review cadence, metric definitions, office outcome updates, and pause authority</td><td>Stage-by-stage totals, source systems, date window, denominators, missing outcomes, and a decision</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Match the campaign settings to the business question</h2>
  <p>
    Ask the campaign manager to show the selected objective, conversion location, conversion event,
    and performance goal, then explain what the system is being asked to produce. Meta's current
    <a href="https://www.facebookblueprint.com/student/path/253165-selecting-objectives-course">campaign-objective and conversion-settings training</a>
    presents these as connected decisions. The label on an objective is not evidence that someone
    booked, attended, or became a patient.
  </p>
  <p>
    Write the practice question next to the setup. It may be whether an approved offer can produce
    relevant inquiries for a location within a declared spending boundary. Save the current
    settings rather than relying on a description from an older campaign, because available
    controls and automated delivery can change.
  </p>

  <h2>Approve the offer, creative, and destination as one path</h2>
  <p>
    Review the copy, on-screen text, image or video, form, landing page, and next step together. The
    practice should verify service descriptions, locations, clinician information, offer terms,
    testimonials, and permissions. The responsible reviewer should also inspect the current
    <a href="https://transparency.meta.com/policies/ad-standards/">Meta Advertising Standards</a>
    and any applicable professional requirements before launch.
  </p>
  <p>
    Save the approved version and name who may replace it. Use the
    <a href="/blog/chiropractic-video-content-approval-workflow/">video content approval workflow</a>
    when a campaign depends on practice recordings. This is an operational review, not a promise
    that a particular phrase or format will be approved or outperform another.
  </p>

  <h2>Choose the destination with the handoff in view</h2>
  <p>
    An instant form, website page, phone call, message, or booking route creates a different first
    record and different failure points. Define the fields, disclosures, notification recipient,
    backup, coverage hours, status owner, and booking destination. Then use an approved non-patient
    test to confirm the contact reaches the intended person and system.
  </p>
  <p>
    A confirmation screen does not prove that the office received or worked the inquiry. Use the
    <a href="/blog/chiropractic-advertising-leads-to-appointments/">lead-to-appointment handoff guide</a>
    to map response, qualification, booking, reminders, and attendance. When phone calls are part
    of the campaign, the <a href="/blog/chiropractic-call-tracking-lead-quality/">call-quality guide</a>
    helps separate connected calls from relevant new inquiries.
  </p>

  <h2>Keep every measurement stage separate</h2>
  <p>
    Start with delivery reported by Meta, then preserve the platform lead or other configured
    event. After that, use the appropriate practice-controlled source for contact, relevance,
    booking, attendance, and any accepted patient or financial outcome. Do not rename a platform
    event to fill a missing downstream field.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Meta advertising measurement stages">
    <table>
      <caption>Evidence chain from ad delivery to a practice outcome</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Stage</th><th scope="col">Likely source</th><th scope="col">Question it answers</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Impression</th><td>Ads Manager</td><td>Was an ad served under the campaign's reporting rules?</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Click, view, or engagement</th><td>Ads Manager</td><td>Was a configured interaction recorded?</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Platform lead or conversion event</th><td>Ads Manager and the selected destination</td><td>Did the event defined in the setup occur?</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Received inquiry</th><td>Form, phone, message, scheduler, or CRM</td><td>Did the practice receive a contact it can work?</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Relevant inquiry</th><td>Practice classification</td><td>Did the contact fit the promoted service, market, and declared definition?</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Booked appointment</th><td>Practice scheduler</td><td>Was an appointment placed on the schedule?</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Held appointment</th><td>Practice attendance record</td><td>Did the appointment occur under the practice's reporting rule?</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Patient or financial outcome</th><td>Approved practice records</td><td>Was a separately defined downstream outcome documented?</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    For every cost or rate, show the numerator, denominator, currency, date window, included costs,
    and attribution rule. Media-only cost per platform lead and total marketing cost per held
    appointment answer different questions. Pending outcomes should remain pending rather than
    being treated as failures or successes.
  </p>

  <h2>Give the practice and agency a shared review table</h2>
  <p>
    A useful review does not ask the advertising account to supply facts that only the office can
    know. Give each stage a source, owner, and correction path.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Meta advertising owner review table">
    <table>
      <caption>Owner review and decision record</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Review item</th><th scope="col">Owner and evidence</th><th scope="col">Possible decision</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Setup</th><td>Campaign manager; current settings and change record</td><td>Keep, correct, or test a defined change</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Creative and offer</th><td>Production owner and practice approver; approved version and permissions</td><td>Keep, revise, replace, or hold</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Lead delivery</th><td>Campaign manager and office lead; labeled test and routing record</td><td>Launch, repair, or pause</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Inquiry quality</th><td>Office lead; aggregate reasons with declared definitions</td><td>Revise message, market, destination, or qualification</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Booking and attendance</th><td>Practice owner; scheduler and attendance records</td><td>Fix handoff, capacity, reminders, or reporting</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Budget</th><td>Practice owner and campaign manager; complete cost scope and dated cohort</td><td>Continue, revise, pause, or consider a bounded increase</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Use the first review to find the constraint</h2>
  <p>
    Set the review date and required evidence before launch. Review earlier when the destination,
    routing, budget, geography, creative approval, or inquiry handoff may be broken. A useful review
    asks whether the campaign produced the event it was configured for, whether the office received
    and classified the inquiries, and where the first material gap appears.
  </p>
  <p>
    Use the <a href="/blog/the-future-of-chiropractic-facebook-marketing-emerging-trends-and-strategies-in-2023/">Facebook
    Ads review-before-scaling guide</a> for a compact campaign audit. If the real question is channel
    fit, compare the job of each platform in the
    <a href="/blog/google-ads-vs-facebook-ads-for-chiropractors/">Google Ads versus Facebook Ads guide</a>.
    No lead-cost threshold, creative format, audience setting, or review period guarantees a
    result across practices.
  </p>

  <h2>Bring the campaign and the office workflow</h2>
  <p>
    Bring your current creative, account access, campaign report, lead route, appointment capacity,
    and office outcome definitions to a <a href="/schedule/">ChiroCandy discovery call</a>. We can map
    what the ad account can show, what the practice must verify, and which responsibility needs to
    be resolved before scaling. The goal is a clear scope and evidence chain—not a promise about
    reach, lead cost, appointments, patients, or revenue.
  </p>
